  "content": {
    "component": "news_article",
    "_uid": "3455-root",
    "title": "Advocating for higher taxes on cigarettes, vapes and nicotine",
    "subtitle": null,
    "summary": "Reducing tobacco amongst the Kenyan youth use for better health",
    "media": [
      {
        "_uid": "b8f844af54bf5033",
        "component": "news_media",
        "role": "banner",
        "filename": "3455-BannerImage.jpg",
        "src": "/news-media/3455-BannerImage.jpg",
        "media_type": "image",
        "caption": null,
        "alt": "Advocating for higher taxes on cigarettes, vapes and nicotine"
      }
    ],
    "body": {
      "type": "doc",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Since joining the WHO Framework Convention on Tobacco Control (FCTC) in 2004 and enacting the Tobacco Control Act in 2007, Kenya has actively worked to reduce tobacco use. However, tobacco use among young Kenyans remains alarmingly high. A 2007 survey found that 1 in 10 students aged 13-15 were smokers, with boys twice as likely as girls to use tobacco. More recent data, including a 2022 study by the Kenya Tobacco Board, shows a rise in e-cigarette and nicotine pouch use among youth, highlighting the need for stronger regulatory and tax policies."
            },
            {
              "type": "hard_break"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Tobacco Control Act is enforced by Kenya's 47 counties, leading to inconsistent application. This fragmented approach has allowed the tobacco industry to exploit regulatory gaps, especially in under-resourced regions, and target young people with tobacco products. The central government needs to ensure a unified and effective implementation of tobacco control measures across all counties."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Kenya's cigarette taxation is also insufficient. As of 2023, the tax on a pack of 20 cigarettes is about one-third of its retail price, far below the WHO's recommendation that taxes should make up 70-75% of the retail price to effectively reduce consumption. Kenya's current tax policy scores just 0.88 out of 5 on the tobacco tax scorecard, falling short of the average for middle-income countries and Africa. The 2017 introduction of a two-tier tax system, which separates cheap from premium cigarettes, has further complicated efforts to reduce smoking. This system encourages smokers to switch to cheaper cigarettes, making it easier for young people to start smoking."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The rise of nicotine pouches presents a new challenge. The tobacco industry claims these products are safer than traditional cigarettes, but there is no independent evidence to support this. In fact, nicotine pouches might even lead to increased cigarette smoking. Their low tax and appealing flavors make them particularly attractive to young people. The current tax rate on nicotine pouches is minimal compared to their retail price, indicating weak regulatory oversight."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Similarly, the taxation of e-cigarette liquids is inadequate. E-liquids are often sold below their taxed value, reflecting poor regulation. Comprehensive measures are needed to manage the entire supply chain of these products and support targeted cessation efforts and awareness campaigns."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Kenya could benefit from adopting a more progressive approach, similar to Uganda's. Uganda has successfully implemented higher tobacco taxes and seen a significant decrease in tobacco use. In contrast, Kenya's tax reforms have been less effective, showing the need for more aggressive taxation policies."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "To protect young Kenyans from tobacco and nicotine-related harms, the government must take decisive action. This includes raising tobacco taxes to meet WHO recommendations, eliminating the two-tier tax system, increasing taxes on nicotine pouches, and improving regulation of e-liquids. Better data collection on tobacco and nicotine use is essential to inform these policies. Additionally, developing a social justice tax framework to hold the tobacco industry accountable for its public health impacts is crucial. By making these changes, Kenya can make significant progress in reducing tobacco use and safeguarding the health of its youth."
            },
            {
              "type": "hard_break"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This article is derived from a ",
